<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Deliberately empty.

  Entitlements are exceptions to the hardened runtime, not a feature list, and
  kettle needs none of them. Notarization requires Developer ID signing, the
  hardened runtime, and a secure timestamp; those are `codesign` flags, not
  entitlements. Specifically NOT claimed here, with the reason each is absent:

    com.apple.security.app-sandbox
        kettle is not sandboxed. A terminal spawns arbitrary user programs and
        reads arbitrary paths; sandboxing it would be theatre.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        Metal shader compilation happens inside Apple's own frameworks, which
        carry their own entitlements. kettle maps no executable memory itself.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        kettle loads no third-party plugin dylibs. The Lua surface is
        interpreted, not native.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
        Nothing needs DYLD_* preserved across the signature boundary.

    com.apple.security.get-task-allow
        Debug-only, and it makes a build ineligible for notarization.

  `forkpty(3)` and process spawning need no entitlement at all.

  If a future change genuinely requires one, add it here WITH the reason, and
  expect the notarization service to scrutinise it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
